<p>As with the majority of the country, the 1920s brought growth and prosperity to Mandan. The most significant event, the first automobile bridge across the Missouri River in the Dakotas in 1922, prompted a surge of commercial development in downtown Mandan. While North Dakota was a "dry" state since it joined the Union in 1889, many told and untold events were associated with alcoholic beverages in Mandan.</p>

<h3>1920</h3>

<p>{% person "h-l-henke" %}H. L. Henke{% endperson %}, president of the Mandan City Commission (i.e., mayor), was narrowly reelected. He defeated two challengers, {% person "a-o-henderson" %}Dr. A. O. Henderson{% endperson %} and {% person "william-peterson" %}William Peterson{% endperson %}. Similarly, the other two open seats on the commission were hotly contested, with only 10 votes separating the top three candidates. {% person "j-b-fredericks" %}J. B. Fredericks{% endperson %} and {% person "william-mcdonald" %}William McDonald{% endperson %} were declared the winners once the vote count was complete.</p>

