<p>In April, {% person "h-h-king-williams", "H. H. “King” Williams" %}H. H. "King" Williams{% endperson %} opened two businesses. He established a cigar stand in the lobby of the Lewis &amp; Clark Hotel to serve travelers arriving from the depot across the street. He had recently returned from the Twin Cities after purchasing plans and machinery for a soda-bottling factory. Production would begin later that year in the former Commercial Hotel building at 316–318 East Main Street.</p>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1920s/288-post-fire-photo.jpg" alt="Historic image from the The Roar of the Twenties 1920-1929 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>On August 20, a fire destroyed the second floor of the Northern Pacific Railway passenger depot on West Main Street. The fire started in the telegraph battery room, likely when faulty wiring ignited hydrogen released by the wet-cell batteries.</p>

<p>Although volunteer firefighters and railroad staff quickly extinguished the fire, the building required major repairs. Local businessmen immediately pushed for construction of an entirely new building to keep pace with the newer depot recently built in Bismarck.</p>

<h3>1921</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1920s/100-ctz-clock.jpg" alt="Historic image from the The Roar of the Twenties 1920-1929 page">
  </figure>
</div>

<p>In April, more than 100 members of the Mandan Commercial Club voted unanimously for the city to make a permanent change from Mountain to Central Time. A committee was appointed to present the proposal to the City Commission.</p>

<p>In August, robbers failed to blow open the vault at the Mandan Post Office despite using nitroglycerin. Only the postmaster's .45-caliber Colt revolver was stolen. He had purchased it just two months earlier, possibly in response to a series of robberies occurring across the nation. The following Monday morning, {% person "frank-roby" %}Frank Roby{% endperson %}, proprietor of the Roby Machine Shop, opened the damaged vault door with an electric drill. {% person "postmaster-foran", "Postmaster Foran" %}Postmaster Foran{% endperson %} found all the money and stamps intact inside. Two employees, {% person "madell-motsiff" %}Miss Madell Motsiff{% endperson %} and {% person "marian-keidel" %}Miss Marian Keidel{% endperson %}, resigned their part-time positions at the post office only days later.</p>

<h3>1922</h3>

<p>The <a href="/places/landmarks/liberty-memorial-bridge/">Liberty Memorial Bridge</a>, the largest project funded by the U.S. Congress in 1919, was dedicated to World War I veterans on September 19. The automobile bridge linked Mandan and Bismarck and completed the first transcontinental highway in the National Parks Highway system that did not require ferry crossings.</p>

