<p>The <a href="/places/landmarks/rough-rider-statue/">Roughrider statue</a> depicting {% person "theodore-roosevelt" %}Theodore Roosevelt{% endperson %} was dedicated on July 2. Dignitaries, including members of the Roosevelt family and high-level politicians from throughout the state, attended. The statue was donated by Mandan's first mayor, {% person "henry-waldo-coe" %}Henry Waldo Coe{% endperson %}, a lifelong friend of Roosevelt.</p>

<h3>1926</h3>

<h3>1929</h3>

<div class="image-gallery">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/area-history/1920s/214-douglas-dt-army-bomber.jpg" alt="Douglas DT U.S. Army bomber">
    <figcaption>Douglas DT U.S. Army bomber</figcaption>
  </figure>
</div>

<p>In March, the threat of spring flooding prompted an unprecedented response. Masses of floating ice filled more than 80 miles of the river channel. Major ice buildups were reported at Harmon, 10 miles north of Mandan, and Huff, 20 miles south. Attempts to break them up with dynamite were unsuccessful. When several northern ice dams broke and joined the jam immediately north of the city, the risk of catastrophe demanded further action.</p>

<p>When the river rose 4 feet in five hours at Sanger, 30 miles north of Mandan, U.S. Army bombers were dispatched with 55 tons of high-explosive bombs to break up the massive ice jams. Fortunately, nature intervened. The river's force eventually cut a new channel west around the southern ice pack. An edge of the ice dam broke off, and a fissure ripped across its entire width, opening the main river channel. Flooding was averted, to the relief of Mandan residents.</p>
